\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.13}{\ignorespaces Bures--Wasserstein and Fisher--Rao covariance geodesics in the $2\times 2$ positive-semidefinite cone. Both panels use the coordinates $u=(a-b)/\sqrt  2$, $v=\sqrt  2c$, $t=(a+b)/\sqrt  2$ for $\Sigma =\left (\begin  {smallmatrix}a&c\\c&b\end  {smallmatrix}\right )$. Each panel shows three intrinsic geodesics, including paths that start close to the cone boundary; faint gray chords show the corresponding ambient Euclidean segments. The Bures geodesics can reach rank-deficient covariances on the boundary, whereas the Fisher--Rao geodesics are drawn toward small positive-definite regularizations. The omitted boundary endpoints correspond to rank-one limiting covariances, which are not at finite Fisher--Rao distance.}}{37}{figure.2.13}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:monge-gaussian-fr-vs-bures-cone}{{2.13}{37}{Bures--Wasserstein and Fisher--Rao covariance geodesics in the $2\times 2$ positive-semidefinite cone. Both panels use the coordinates $u=(a-b)/\sqrt 2$, $v=\sqrt 2c$, $t=(a+b)/\sqrt 2$ for $\Sigma =\left (\begin {smallmatrix}a&c\\c&b\end {smallmatrix}\right )$. Each panel shows three intrinsic geodesics, including paths that start close to the cone boundary; faint gray chords show the corresponding ambient Euclidean segments. The Bures geodesics can reach rank-deficient covariances on the boundary, whereas the Fisher--Rao geodesics are drawn toward small positive-definite regularizations. The omitted boundary endpoints correspond to rank-one limiting covariances, which are not at finite Fisher--Rao distance}{figure.2.13}{}}
